// Practice quiz: Primitive types and variables
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/java-basics/primitive-types-and-variables

::o1 choose a type::A program stores the number of bytes in a 5 GB file, 5000000000. Which type fits best? {
=long#Correct. 5000000000 is a whole number beyond int's limit of 2147483647, so it needs long, written 5000000000L.
~int#Not quite. int stops at 2147483647, so 5000000000 is too large and will not compile as an int literal.
~double#Not quite. A byte count is a whole number; double is for values with a fractional part.
~char#Not quite. char holds a single character, not a large number.
}

::o1 char or String::Which declaration stores the single letter Y correctly? {
=char answer \= 'Y';#Correct. A char holds exactly one character, written in single quotes.
~char answer \= "Y";#Not quite. Double quotes make a String; a char literal uses single quotes.
~boolean answer \= 'Y';#Not quite. A boolean holds only true or false.
~int answer \= Y;#Not quite. Y without quotes is read as a variable name, and int is for numbers.
}

::o2 integer division::What is the value of 9 / 4 in Java? {
=2#Correct. Both operands are int, so the division drops the fraction.
~2.25#Not quite. That needs a double operand, such as 9 / 4.0.
~3#Not quite. Integer division does not round up; it drops the fraction.
~2.0#Not quite. int divided by int gives an int, which has no .0 part.
}

::o2 narrowing cast::What is the value of (int) 9.99? {
=9#Correct. Casting a double to int keeps the whole-number part and drops the fraction.
~10#Not quite. The cast does not round; it truncates.
~9.99#Not quite. The cast changes the type to int, which cannot hold a fraction.
~A compile error#Not quite. An explicit cast is how you tell the compiler you accept the narrowing.
}

::o3 declaration or assignment::Which line is an assignment, not a declaration? {
=total \= total + 5;#Correct. No type comes first, so it changes the value of an existing variable.
~int total \= 0;#Not quite. A type before the name makes this a declaration.
~var total \= 0;#Not quite. var declares a new variable and lets the compiler infer its type.
~double total;#Not quite. This declares a variable without giving it a value yet.
}

::o3 when to use var::Which use of var keeps the type obvious to a reader? {
=var name \= "Ana";#Correct. The right side is a String literal, so the inferred type is obvious.
~var x \= calculate();#Not quite. A reader must look up calculate to know the type; write the type instead.
~var n;#Not quite. var needs a starting value to infer from, so this does not compile.
~var big \= 7 / 2;#Not quite. The type is int, but readers can easily misread it as a fraction; an explicit type is clearer.
}
